*.sh text eol=lf
scripts/hooks/* text eol=lf

# Apache vhosts are deployed to a Linux box and byte-compared there by CD.
# CRLF would make every deploy see a spurious change and reload Apache needlessly.
deploy/apache/*.conf text eol=lf
deploy/apache/*.example text eol=lf

# Staging compose is byte-compared on a Linux box by CD; CRLF would fake a change.
deploy/staging/*.yml text eol=lf

# core#1076. Same reason as deploy/apache/ above, one directory over: everything in
# deploy/server/ is hand-installed onto a Linux box (core#747), and two of these .conf
# files are pinned byte-identical to their deploy/apache/ twins by
# tests/test_deploy/test_server_script_coverage.py.
#
# 🚨 Without these rules that guard is red on EVERY Windows push and green in CI: the
# blobs are the same git object, `core.autocrlf=true` materialises deploy/server/ as
# CRLF and deploy/apache/ as LF, and the guard compares raw bytes. Linux CI checks out
# both as LF, so it passes there for a reason unrelated to the property it asserts.
# `deploy/server/*.sh` was already covered by the `*.sh` rule at the top, which is why
# only the .conf/.yml half drifted.
deploy/server/*.conf text eol=lf
deploy/server/*.yml text eol=lf
